## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a secure, cost-conscious AWS-native pipeline that handles PII responsibly using services like S3, Lambda, Comprehend, and DynamoDB, with proper error handling and auditing.

## Key Concepts
- PII detection: Amazon Comprehend PII entities
- Data flow: S3 -> Lambda -> S3 (redacted) -> DynamoDB
- Privacy controls: encryption at rest (SSE/KMS), data minimization
- Reliability: dead-letter queues (DLQ), retries, idempotent processing, logging

## Code Example
```javascript
const AWS = require('aws-sdk');
const s3 = new AWS.S3();
const comprehend = new AWS.Comprehend({region: 'us-east-1'});
async function redact(text) {
  const res = await comprehend.detectPiiEntities({ Text: text, LanguageCode: 'en' }).promise();
  let redacted = text;
  // naive approach: replace ranges from end to start to avoid offset shifts
  const ranges = res.Entities.map(e => ({ s: e.BeginOffset, e: e.EndOffset }));
  ranges.sort((a,b) => b.s - a.s);
  for (const r of ranges) {
    redacted = redacted.substring(0, r.s) + '[REDACTED]' + redacted.substring(r.e);
  }
  return redacted;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate redaction accuracy and handle false positives/negatives? 
- How would you adapt this for higher throughput or multilingual transcripts?

## Why This Is Asked
Assess ability to design a scalable, multi-tenant AI moderation pipeline on AWS with policy-driven routing, data isolation, and compliant audit logging.

## Key Concepts
- SageMaker multi-model endpoints and per-tenant routing
- DynamoDB for policy storage; S3 for audit logs and artifacts
- Tenant isolation via prefixes, IAM scoping, KMS encryption
- Step Functions for orchestration, error handling, retries
- Cost controls: autoscaling, model cache, off-peak scheduling

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-routing logic for tenant-aware inference
function routeToEndpoint(tenantId, itemType) {
  const policy = fetchPolicy(tenantId)
  if (itemType === 'image') return { endpoint: policy.imageEndpoint, threshold: policy.imageThreshold }
  return { endpoint: policy.textEndpoint, threshold: policy.textThreshold }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test tenant policy overrides without affecting others?
- How would you enforce data deletion and retention across S3 and DynamoDB?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests end-to-end design for long-form audio with multi-chapter outputs, requiring integration of Transcribe, SageMaker, OpenSearch, and DynamoDB, plus governance, privacy, and cross-account considerations.

## Key Concepts

- AWS Transcribe: language auto-detect, diarization
- Time-based chunking of transcripts
- SageMaker endpoint for per-chapter summaries
- OpenSearch and DynamoDB indexing for chapter search
- S3 storage with timestamped prefixes and encryption
- Data lineage with Glue and auditing/tracking
- Cross-account access, IAM least-privilege, DLQ, cost controls

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-code: chunk transcript, call SageMaker per chapter, write outputs
async function processChapteredTranscript(transcript) {
  const chapters = chunkByTime(transcript);
  for (const c of chapters) {
    const summary = await sageMaker.summarize(c.text);
    await s3.put(`episode/${transcript.episodeId}/${c.start}-${c.end}.txt`, c.text);
    await s3.put(`episode/${transcript.episodeId}/${c.start}-${c.end}.summary.txt`, summary);
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How do you ensure idempotent re-processing for re-uploaded files?
- What operational metrics and alerts would you set for cost and quality drift?
